  "observations": [
    "The reference matches Jugendlinna, the Turku City Office at Kristiinankatu 1: the supplied photograph title and building identity agree with the distinctive yellow-rendered riverside facade, grey stone piers, paired rooftop turrets and green copper roof. Reference verification uses the supplied photograph and metadata.",
    "Only the large Jugendstil building is parameterized. The low pale building and red wooden building to its right, and the white modern building to its left, are excluded.",
    "Supplied height evidence gives base 3.512 m N2000 and top 29.900 m N2000, yielding 26.388 m above base. This is the overall calibration, not a measured main cornice height. Its correspondence to individual roof and turret peaks is uncertain; flagpoles are excluded from visual height estimates.",
    "All other metre values are approximate visual estimates, with dimensional confidence about 0.5. Main eaves are approximately 18–19 m above base; the main ridge and turret caps occupy the approximately 24–26.4 m zone.",
    "Main wall material is warm pale ochre render, with extensive grey stone facing at the ground storey, vertical piers and central turret-bearing bays. Projecting bays, carved ornament and horizontal cornices articulate the facade.",
    "brick.colour represents the render albedo. Concealed brick dimensions, mortar and running bond are low-confidence compatibility estimates, not observed exposed masonry; do not draw a brick pattern over the render.",
    "Stone facing combines smooth ashlar and rough-faced panels. The specified stone size is a representative block estimate; actual courses and block lengths vary substantially.",
    "The primary body has round-arched ground-floor openings and three visible tiers of rectangular upper windows. Upper-window heights and grouped bay arrangements vary, so rows_m is intentionally empty. The rectangular window parameters represent a typical upper-floor opening only.",
    "Typical upper windows have one principal vertical mullion, transoms and additional small upper panes. Mullion counts and spacing vary by bay; spacing_m is an approximate centre-to-centre interval within regular bays.",
    "Small rounded dormers belong to the roof and oval openings belong to the central turret-bearing bays. Neither is included in global window rows.",
    "The broad hipped roof has strongly patinated green copper sheets and visible standing seams, consistent with the supplied copper roof tag. Small projecting bay roofs are also green; turret caps appear much darker and their precise finish is uncertain.",
    "Two narrow integrated rooftop turrets are visible, rather than one freestanding tower. Tower parameters approximate one turret-bearing bay and cannot encode the paired arrangement. minimum_height_m and maximum_width_m are classification thresholds, not measured overall dimensions.",
    "stone_top_m and copper_start_m approximate the transition from pale stone turret bases to dark sheet-metal caps. opening_rows is empty because turret openings cannot be measured reliably. The oval features are windows; no clock is identifiable, so clock dimensions are zero.",
    "Entrance dimensions approximate a visible riverside arched doorway. The photograph does not securely identify the principal entrance. The southeast orientation is inferred from the riverside location; flags partly obscure doorway details.",
    "Colours are estimated neutral albedo in 0–255 RGB, with confidence about 0.7. Weathering and patina are visual estimates; seam spacing and masonry unit dimensions have confidence about 0.35.",
    "Reviewed (issue #2, 2026-11-13 review): both street fronts carry a granite-faced ground storey with round-arched openings and granite vertical fields and twin tower bays; the end bays, the corner and the top storey are cream render. Courtyard and back walls are plain render with the upper storeys' windows."
